The '''Porsche 918 Spyder''' is a limited-production mid-engine plug-in-hybrid [[sports car]] manufactured by German marque [[Porsche]].<ref>{{cite web |publisher=Top Gear |author=Paul Horrell |url=http://www.topgear.com/car-reviews/porsche/918-spyder/weissach-pack/first-drive-0 |title=First Drive: Porsche 918 Spyder Weissach Pack |date=26 November 2013}}</ref> The 918 Spyder is a [[plug-in hybrid]] powered by a [[Mid-engine design|mid]]-mounted [[naturally aspirated]] {{cvt|4593|cc|L|1|order=flip}} [[Porsche V8 engines#Type M18|V8 engine]], developing {{cvt|447|kW|PS hp}} at 8,700{{nbsp}}RPM, with two [[electric motor]]s delivering an additional {{cvt|210|kW|PS hp|0}} for a combined output of {{cvt|652|kW|PS hp|0|order=}} and {{cvt|1280|Nm|0}} of torque.<ref name="AMS">{{cite web|url=http://www.auto-motor-und-sport.de/supertest/porsche-918-spyder-im-supertest-der-herr-des-rings-8375698.html |title=Porsche 918 Spyder im Supertest | publisher=Sport auto| author=Horst von Saurma |date=28 June 2014|access-date=17 July 2018}}</ref><ref name="918 Press" /> The 918 Spyder's 6.8{{nbsp}}kWh [[lithium-ion battery]] pack delivers an all-electric range of {{Convert|12|mi|abbr=on|order=flip}} under the [[US Environmental Protection Agency]]'s five-cycle tests.<ref name=EPA2015spyder/>

Production began on 18 September 2013, with deliveries initially scheduled to begin in December 2013, and a starting price of ≈€781,000 (US$845,000 or £711,000).<ref name=AB1012/><ref name=Prod0918>{{cite web|url=http://insideevs.com/porsche-918-spyder-officially-enters-production/|title=Porsche 918 Spyder Officially Enters Production|author=Eric Loveday|publisher=InsideEVs.com|date=18 September 2013|access-date=19 September 2013}}</ref><ref name="TopSpeed 2014-10">{{cite web|last=Cupler|first=Justin|title=2013 Porsche 918 Spyder|url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/porsche/2013-porsche-918-spyder-martini-racing-prototype/|publisher=TopSpeed|access-date=11 November 2012|date=30 October 2012}}</ref> The 918 Spyder was sold out in December 2014 and production ended in June 2015.<ref name=EndProd/>

The 918 Spyder was first shown as a concept at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2010|80th Geneva Motor Show]] in March 2010.<ref name=AB1012/> On 28 July 2010, after 2,000 declarations of interest, the [[supervisory board]] of Porsche AG approved series development of the 918 Spyder.<ref name=PorschePressRelease>{{cite press release|url=http://www.porsche.com/usa/aboutporsche/pressreleases/pag/?pool=international-de&id=2010-07-28 |title=Porsche Presents 918 Spyder High-Performance Concept Sports Car in Geneva |publisher=[[Porsche AG]] |date=28 July 2010 |access-date=28 July 2010}}</ref> The production version was unveiled at the [[Frankfurt Motor Show#2013|September 2013 Frankfurt Motor Show]].<ref name=Frankfurt/> Porsche also unveiled the RSR racing variant of the 918 at the [[North American International Auto Show#2011|2011 North American International Auto Show]], which combined hybrid technology first used in the [[Porsche 911 GT3#997 GT3 R Hybrid (2010–2011)|997 GT3 R Hybrid]], with styling from the 918 Spyder.<ref name="RSR">{{cite press release |url= http://www.porsche.com/usa/aboutporsche/pressreleases/pag/?pool=international-de&id=2011-01-10 |title=Porsche 918 RSR – racing laboratory with even higher-performance hybrid drive |date=10 January 2010 |access-date=17 September 2011 |publisher=[[Porsche AG]]}}</ref> However, the 918 RSR did not make it to production. The 918 Spyder was the second [[plug-in hybrid]] car manufactured by Porsche, after the 2014 [[Porsche Panamera#Panamera S E-Hybrid|Panamera S E-Hybrid]].<ref name="porsche PR 2010-03-02">{{cite press release |url=http://www.porsche.com/usa/aboutporsche/pressreleases/pag/?pool=international-de&id=2010-03-02 |title=Porsche Presents 918 Spyder High-Performance Concept Sports Car in Geneva |date=2 March 2010 |access-date=7 March 2010 |publisher=[[Porsche AG]]}}</ref>

Since its release, the Porsche 918 Spyder has been the subject of half a dozen different recalls, for issues ranging from both front and rear control arms breaking to seat belts.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Cars.com |url=https://www.cars.com/research/porsche-918_spyder/recalls/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112015046/https://www.cars.com/research/porsche-918_spyder/recalls/ |archive-date=2020-11-12 |access-date=2025-10-15 |website=Cars.com}}</ref>

==Specifications==
[[File:Porsche 918 sports car.jpg|thumb|left|Rear view]]
[[File:Porsche 9R6 RS Spyder - engine, gearbox and rear axle.jpg|thumb|left|The 918 Spyder's engine is based on the unit used in the Porsche RS Spyder]]
The 918 Spyder is powered by a {{cvt|4593|cc|L CID|1}} naturally aspirated V8 engine built on the same architecture as the one used in the [[Porsche RS Spyder|RS Spyder]]<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://www.scribd.com/document/811499667/2015-918 |title=Service Information 2015 Technik Introduction 918 Spyder |publisher=[[Porsche]] |via=[[Scribd]]}}</ref> [[Le Mans Prototype]] racing car without any engine belts.

In developing the V8 for the 918 Spyder the bore remained 95&nbsp;mm with the stroke increased from 59.9&nbsp;mm (for 3397 cc) to 81&nbsp;mm (for 4593 cc). The engine ran to 9,200 rpm. In 2011 an RSR version was introduced, which offered 563&nbsp;bhp at 10,300 rpm.

The engine weighs {{cvt|135|kg}}, according to Porsche, and delivers {{cvt|447|kW|PS hp|0}} at 8,700{{nbsp}}RPM and {{cvt|540|Nm|0}} of maximum torque at 6,700{{nbsp}}RPM.<ref name="918 Press">{{cite press release |title=Press Information 918 Spyder |date=2013 |publisher=[[Porsche AG]] |url=https://presse.porsche.de/presskits_until_2015/products/2013/spyder/text/presskit/918_Spyder_Fahrvorstellung_EN6_hp.pdf |access-date=21 July 2019 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190721105445/https://presse.porsche.de/presskits_until_2015/products/2013/spyder/text/presskit/918_Spyder_Fahrvorstellung_EN6_hp.pdf |archive-date=2019-07-21}}</ref> This is supplemented by two electric motors delivering an additional {{convert|210|kW|PS hp|0|abbr=on}}. One {{cvt|115|kW|PS hp|0}} electric motor drives the rear wheels in parallel with the engine and also serves as the main generator. This motor and engine deliver power to the rear axle via a 7-speed gearbox coupled to Porsche's own [[Dual-clutch transmission#Porsche (VW GROUP)|PDK double-clutch system]]. The front {{cvt|95|kW|PS hp|0}} electric motor directly drives the front axle; an electric clutch decouples the motor when not in use. The total system delivers {{cvt|887|PS|kW PS hp|0|order=out}} and {{cvt|1280|Nm|0}} of torque.<ref name="918 Press" /> Porsche provided official performance figures of {{cvt|0{{ndash}}100|km/h}} in 2.6 seconds, {{cvt|0{{ndash}}200|km/h|0}} in 7.2 seconds, {{cvt|0{{ndash}}300|km/h|0}} in 19.9 seconds and a top speed of {{cvt|345|km/h|0}}.<ref name="AMS"/><ref name="TopSpeed 2014-10" /><ref>{{cite web|last=Wolfcale|first=James|title=Final numbers for the 918 Spyder: 0-60 in 2.6 seconds|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/news/a6009/porsche-918-performance-numbers/ |publisher=[[Road & Track]] |access-date=23 August 2017 |date=18 November 2013}}</ref><ref name="MT first ride">{{cite web|last=Febbo|first=Michael|title=Porsche 918 Spyder First Ride|url=http://www.motortrend.com/roadtests/alternative/1210_porsche_918_spyder_first_ride/viewall.html|publisher=Motor Trend|access-date=23 August 2017|date=11 October 2012}}</ref>

Those numbers were surpassed in independent tests which yielded 2.5 seconds for 0{{ndash}}100&nbsp;km/h, 7.0 seconds for 0{{ndash}}200&nbsp;km/h, 19.1 seconds for 0{{ndash}}300&nbsp;km/h, a top speed of {{cvt|352
|km/h|1}} and 17.75 seconds for the standing kilometre reaching a speed of {{cvt|295.9|km/h|mph|1}}.<ref>Auto Bild Sportscars 6/2017</ref><ref>Auto 05/2015 https://www.germancarforum.com/attachments/11211770_682476881864049_1126760535_o-jpg.333918/</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://carbuzz.com/news/watch-the-porsche-918-spyder-max-out-at-218-mph|title=Watch the Porsche 918 Spyder Max Out at 218 MPH|date=3 March 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.auto-motor-und-sport.de/test/porsche-918-spyder/|title=Porsche 918 Spyder: Hybridsportwagen im Test|date=June 2014}}</ref>

In ''Car and Driver''{{'}}s independent test of the Porsche 918 they achieved {{cvt|0{{ndash}}60|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 2.2 seconds,{{efn-lr|2.1 seconds with 1-foot rollout<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/features/g30083537/quickest-cars-of-the-decade/?slide=21 |title=The Quickest Cars of the Decade (with 1-foot rollout) |first=Connor |last=Hoffman |date=7 December 2019 |access-date=3 September 2020 |work=Car and Driver}}</ref>}} {{cvt|0{{ndash}}100|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 4.9 seconds, {{cvt|0{{ndash}}180|mph|0|abbr=on}} in 17.5 seconds, and the quarter mile in 9.8 seconds.{{efn-lr|9.7 seconds with 1-foot rollout<ref name="918CDUpdate">{{Cite web|first=C.K.|last=Colwell|title=2020 in Review: Testing Winners and Losers |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/features/a34900893/2020-winners-and-losers-testing-edition/ |access-date=2021-01-24|work=Car and Driver |date=20 December 2020|language=en}}</ref>}}<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.caranddriver.com/features/the-2015-porsche-918-spyder-is-the-quickest-road-car-in-the-world-feature-performance-data-and-complete-specs-page-2|title = Performance Data and Complete Specs|date = 25 July 2014|access-date = 14 September 2014|website = Car and Driver}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=16 May 2014 |title=Porsche 918 Test Sheet -- Car and Driver |url=http://media.caranddriver.com/files/2015-porsche-918-spyder-feature-car-and-driver2015-porsche-918-spyder.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021121834/http://media.caranddriver.com/files/2015-porsche-918-spyder-feature-car-and-driver2015-porsche-918-spyder.pdf |archive-date=2014-10-21 |website=[[Car and Driver]]}}</ref> In ''[[Motor Trend]]''{{'}}s independent test the Porsche 918 set a production-car track record at Willow Springs Raceway. With a time of 2.4 seconds, it was the fastest car to 60&nbsp;mph that they had ever tested. It stopped from {{convert|60|mph|0|abbr=on}} in {{cvt|94|ft}}, and broke Motor Trend's figure 8 record at 22.2 seconds.<ref name="Motortrend-918Test"/>

The energy storage system is a 312-cell, liquid-cooled 6.8{{nbsp}}kWh [[lithium-ion battery]] positioned behind the passenger cell. In addition to a plug-in charge port at the passenger-side B pillar, the batteries are also charged by [[regenerative braking]] and by excess output from the engine when the car is coasting. {{CO2}} emissions are 79{{nbsp}}g/km and fuel consumption is {{cvt|3|L/100 km}} under the [[New European Driving Cycle]] (NEDC).<ref name="porsche PR 2010-03-02" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Porsche's Plug-in Hybrid 918 Spyder Goes From Dream to Reality|url=http://press.porsche.com/news/release.php?id=637|website=press.porsche.com|publisher=Porsche|access-date=7 October 2014|archive-date=11 October 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141011091133/http://press.porsche.com/news/release.php?id=637}}</ref> The [[U.S. Environmental Protection Agency]] (EPA) under its five-cycle tests rated the 2015 model year Porsche 918 Spyder energy consumption in all-electric mode at 50{{nbsp}}kWh per 100 miles, which translates into a combined city/highway fuel economy of {{cvt|3.5|L/100 km}}. When powered only by the gasoline engine, EPA's official combined city/highway fuel economy is {{cvt|22|mpgus}}.<ref name=EPA2015spyder>{{cite web|url=http://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/Find.do?action=sbs&id=34856|title=2015 Porsche 918 Spyder|publisher=Fueleconomy.gov|author=[[U.S. Environmental Protection Agency]] and [[U.S. Department of Energy]]|date=30 May 2014|access-date= 2 June 2014}}</ref>

The 4.6 litre V8 petrol engine can recharge an empty battery using about one litre of fuel.<ref name=MSN>{{cite web|last=Chilton|first=Chris|title=Porsche 918 Spyder review (2013 onwards)|url=http://cars.uk.msn.com/reviews/porsche-918-spyder-review-2013-onwards|work=MSN Cars|publisher=Microsoft|access-date=3 March 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131130055359/http://cars.uk.msn.com/reviews/porsche-918-spyder-review-2013-onwards|archive-date=30 November 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> The supplied Porsche Universal Charger requires seven hours to charge the battery on a typical 120{{nbsp}}V household AC socket or two hours on a 240{{nbsp}}V charger. A DC charging station can restore the battery to full capacity in 25 minutes.<ref name="Press Release">{{cite web|title=Introducing the Porsche 918 Spyder|url=http://press.porsche.com/news/release.php?id=787|work=Porsche Press Release|publisher=Porsche Cars North America, Inc.|access-date=3 March 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140104212808/http://press.porsche.com/news/release.php?id=787|archive-date=4 January 2014}}</ref>

The 918 Spyder offers five different running modes: E-Drive allows the car to run under battery power alone, using the rear electric motor and front motor, giving a range of {{convert|18|mi|order=flip}} for the concept model.<ref name="Press Release" /> The official U.S. EPA all-electric range is {{cvt|12|mi}}. The total range with a full tank of gasoline and a fully charged battery is {{convert|420|mi}} according to EPA tests.<ref name=EPA2015spyder/> Under the E-Drive mode the car can attain a maximum speed of {{cvt|93|mph|order=flip}}.<ref name=AB1012/> Two hybrid modes (Hybrid, and Race) use both the engine and electric motors to provide the desired levels of economy and performance. In Race mode a push-to-pass button initiates the Hot Lap setting, which delivers additional electrical power.<ref name=Economist>{{cite news |date=20 March 2010|title=Electric supercars: Highly charged motoring  |newspaper= [[The Economist]]|volume=394 |issue=8674 |pages=85–86 |url=https://www.economist.com/science-technology/displaystory.cfm?story_id=15717208 |access-date=25 March 2010}}</ref> The chassis is a [[Carbon fiber reinforced polymer|carbon-fibre-reinforced plastic]] [[monocoque]] and the brake system is boosted electrically (rather than the traditional vacuum boost).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/news/a6358/first-look-tesla-model-s-p85d-dual-motor/|title=Tesla Model S P85D: Dual motors, AWD, 691 hp, 3.2 to 60|author=Chris Cantle|work=Road & Track|date=9 October 2014|access-date=18 April 2015}}</ref>

==Sales and production==
The production version was unveiled at the [[2013 Frankfurt Motor Show]].<ref name=Frankfurt>{{cite web|url=https://www.ausmotive.com/2013/09/10/frankfurt-2013-porsche-918-spyder.html |title=Frankfurt 2013: Porsche 917 Spyder|publisher=AUSmotive.com |date=10 December 2013|access-date=10 September 2013}}</ref> The 918 Spyder was produced in a limited series of 918 units and it was developed in [[Weissach]] and assembled in [[Zuffenhausen]] for the 2014 model year. Production for the 2014 model year started on 18 September 2013<nowiki>,</nowiki> with deliveries scheduled to begin in December 2013.<ref name=AB1012>{{cite web|url=http://www.autoblog.com/2012/10/01/2014-porsche-918-spyder-deep-dive-review/|title=2014 Porsche 918 Spyder |author=Michael Harley |publisher=[[Autoblog.com]]|date=1 October 2012 |access-date=14 October 2012}}</ref><ref name=Prod0918/> Sales in the United States began in June 2014.<ref name=NYT092013>{{cite news|url=http://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/09/10/frankfurt-motor-show-the-record-breaking-porsche-918-spyder-has-arrived/?ref=automobiles&_r=0|title=Frankfurt Motor Show: The Record-Breaking Porsche 918 Spyder Has Arrived|author=Jerry Garrett|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=10 September 2013|access-date=11 September 2013}}</ref> Pricing for the 918 Spyder started at {{euro|781,000}} in Europe and {{USD|845,000}} in the U.S.<ref name="TopSpeed 2014-10" /> According to its battery size, the 918 Spyder was eligible to a [[Electric motor vehicles tax credit in the United States|federal tax credit]] of up to {{USD|3,667}}.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/cars/federal-tax-credits-for-plug-in-hybrids-electric-cars-what-you-need-to-know/2014/08/20/0ae02718-2886-11e4-8b10-7db129976abb_story.html|title=Federal Tax Credits For Plug-In Hybrids, Electric Cars: What You Need To Know|author=Bengt Halvorson|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=20 August 2014|access-date=20 August 2014}}</ref>

Production ended in June 2015 as scheduled.<ref name=EndProd>{{cite web|url=http://www.greencarcongress.com/2015/06/20150619-918spyder.html |title=Porsche finishes production of the 918 Spyder plug-in hybrid; platform for advanced development for vehicles and manufacturing|author=Mike Millikin|publisher=[[Green Car Congress]]|date=19 June 2015|access-date=19 June 2015}}</ref>

The country with the most orders is the United States with 297 units, followed by China and Germany with approximately 100 orders each, and Canada ordering 35 units.<ref name=SoldOut>{{cite press release|url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/porsche-exceeds-previous-years-sales-175900089.html |title=Porsche exceeds previous year's sales by November - Last unit of the 918 Spyder sold |author=Porsche Cars North America |publisher=[[Yahoo! News]] |location=Atlanta |date=10 December 2014 |access-date=12 December 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141216022835/https://finance.yahoo.com/news/porsche-exceeds-previous-years-sales-175900089.html |archive-date=16 December 2014 }}</ref>

According to [[JATO Dynamics]], a total of 105 units have been registered worldwide during the first nine months of 2014.<ref name=JATO092014>{{cite web|url=http://dailykanban.com/2014/12/ev-sales-rise-jato-counts-4000-fewer-model-s-tesla/|title=While EV sales rise, JATO counts 4,000 fewer Model S than Tesla |author=Bertel Schmitt|publisher=Daily Kanban |date=3 December 2014|access-date=6 December 2014}}</ref> The United States is the leading market with 202 units delivered up to May 2015.<ref name=NA918Spyder>{{cite web|url=https://www.goodcarbadcar.net/2014/03/porsche-918-spyder-sales-figures-usa-canada.html|title=Porsche 918 Spyder|author=Timothy Cain|publisher=Good Car Bad Car|date=June 2015|access-date=20 June 2015}}</ref> {{As of|2014|10}}, a total of 9 units were registered in Switzerland, 6 in the Netherlands, 5 units in Canada, 4 in Sweden, 3 in Brazil and 1 in South Africa.<ref name=SalesSwiss2013_14>{{cite web|url=http://www.auto-schweiz.ch/statistiken/autoverkaeufe-nach-modellen/|title=Autoverkäufe nach Modellen - Modellstatistik|language=de|trans-title=Passenger cars by model - Statistics by model|author=Vereinigung Schweizer Automobil-Importeure|publisher=Auto Schweiz Suisse|access-date=6 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140202205708/http://www.auto-schweiz.ch/statistiken/autoverkaeufe-nach-modellen/|archive-date=2 February 2014|url-status=dead}} ''Under "Modellstatistik" download the xls file "Januar - Oktober 2014" for 2014 sales.''</ref><ref name=RAISales102014>{{cite web|url=http://www.raivereniging.nl/artikel/marktinformatie/actuele-verkoopcijfers/verkoopstatistieken.html|title=Actuele verkoopcijfers|language=nl|trans-title=Current sales|author=RAI|publisher=RAI Vereniging|date=24 November 2014|access-date=6 December 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150112014446/http://www.raivereniging.nl/artikel/marktinformatie/actuele-verkoopcijfers/verkoopstatistieken.html|archive-date=12 January 2015}} ''Download the pdf file for detailed sales by model during the first ten months of 2014: "[http://www.raivereniging.nl/ecm/?id=workspace://SpacesStore/9b3baeb1-4a83-48de-aee6-2ead143ca86b nieuwverkoop personenautos 201410] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170823205151/http://www.raivereniging.nl/ecm/?id=workspace%3A%2F%2FSpacesStore%2F9b3baeb1-4a83-48de-aee6-2ead143ca86b |date=23 August 2017 }}".''</ref><ref name=NA918Spyder/><ref name=SwedenSales102014>{{cite web|url=http://www.bilsweden.se/statistik/arkiv-nyregistrering_per_manad_2014/nyregistreringar-oktober-2014|title=Nyregistreringar oktober 2014|language=sv|trans-title=New registrations in October 2014|author=Bil Sweden|publisher=Bil Sweden|date=3 November 2014|access-date=4 October 2014}} ''Download file "PressRel1410.pdf" see table: "Nyregistrerade miljöpersonbilar oktober 2014" with summary of plug-in passenger car sales by model between January and October 2014.''</ref>

== Weissach Package ==
[[File:2014 Porsche 918 Spyder - Weissach Package.jpg|left|thumb|Porsche 918 Spyder with Weissach Package and Salzburg livery]]The Weissach Package is a weight saving and aerodynamics package offered by Porsche for the 918 Spyder. It was an $84,000 factory option in the US. The package included [[magnesium wheels]] specially manufactured by [[BBS Kraftfahrzeugtechnik|BBS]], an extended rear diffuser, and interior parts covered in [[Alcantara (material)|Alcantara]] instead of leather. The windscreen frame, roof, rear wings, and rear-view mirrors were also made out of carbon fiber.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Bonhams : 2015 Porsche 918 'Weissach' SpyderVIN. WPOCA2A18FS800846|url=https://www.bonhams.com//auctions/25221/lot/136/?category=list&length=100000&page=1|access-date=2021-02-24|website=www.bonhams.com}}</ref>

==Porsche 918 RSR==
[[File:Porsche 198 RSR - Flickr - J.Smith831.jpg|thumb|The 918 RSR]]
At the [[North American International Auto Show#2011|2011 North American International Auto Show]] in Detroit, Porsche unveiled the RSR racing variant of the 918 Spyder. Instead of using plug-in hybrid technology, power for the two electric motors is provided by a flywheel accumulator [[KERS]] system that sits beside the driver in the passenger compartment. The V8 is a further development of the direct injection engine from the RS Spyder race car developing {{cvt|414|kW|PS hp}} at 10,300{{nbsp}}RPM. The electric motors each provide an additional {{cvt|150|kW|PS hp}}, giving a peak power output of {{cvt|564|kW|PS hp}}. The six-speed gearbox is a development of the unit from the RS Spyder.<ref name="RSR NL">{{cite web |title=Porsche 918 RSR – hybride racelab met eerbetoon aan Gijs van Lennep |url=https://nieuws.porsche.nl/porsche-918-rsr--hybride-racelab-met-eerbetoon-aan-gijs-van-lennep/ |website=porsche.nl |access-date=21 July 2019 |language=nl}}</ref>

==Nürburgring lap time record==
In September 2013 a 918 fitted with the optional 'Weissach Package' set a [[Nürburgring]] lap time of 6:57 on the [[list of Nürburgring Nordschleife lap times|{{cvt|12.8|mi|order=flip}} road course]], reducing the previous record by 14 seconds, and making it the first street-legal production car to break the 7-minute barrier.<ref name=NYT092013/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ausringers.com/2013/09/10/porsche-918-spyder-breaks-7-minute-barrier.html |title=Porsche 918 Spyder breaks 7 minute barrier|publisher=AUSringers.com |date=10 September 2013 |access-date=10 September 2013}}</ref>

